<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Senate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as U.S. Senate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Senate is the upper house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a>, established by the constitution of 1787 and first convened in 1789, with two senators from each state. Its approval by a two-thirds majority is required to ratify treaties, giving it a decisive voice over agreements such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hay-bunau-varilla-treaty">Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a>. In 1902 it voted for the Panama route over Nicaragua for an interoceanic canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-senate.jpg" alt="United States Senate" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Dreka designed the actual seal, first used in 1885 per here. Vectorized from a version in stained glass.; Original:; Maksim; Vector … · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_United_States_Senate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
